WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:02.800
Your numbers now reconcile
to one revenue truth.

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:04.600
Now do the same for your words.

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:08.100
A message that promises a result
nobody can prove is a liability.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:11.267
Here, every claim is checked
against proof you approved.

00:00:11.333 --> 00:00:14.700
In this clip you'll set your voice
and the claims you never make.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:18.167
You'll build a proof library, choose
what happens to an unbacked claim,

00:00:18.167 --> 00:00:20.167
and mine your customers' own words.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:20.933
Without a gate,

00:00:20.933 --> 00:00:24.433
Orbit drafts a first touch that says
Northwind doubled their pipeline.

00:00:24.433 --> 00:00:27.467
Nobody approved that number, and
Northwind never agreed to be named.

00:00:27.467 --> 00:00:31.233
Say only what you can prove, checked
when proposed and again when sent.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:34.267
First, voice. In the console:
Settings, Brand and compliance.

00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:36.133
Name the voice and give it tone words.

00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:39.067
List forbidden claims, the
promises your brand never makes,

00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:41.267
each with a reason that
shows on a blocked draft.

00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:43.000
Add words to avoid and
a few voice samples.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.933
Drafts imitate their
style and never copy them.

00:00:45.933 --> 00:00:47.733
When you save, the rules are signed,

00:00:47.733 --> 00:00:50.833
and you can paste any draft into
the checker to see its violations.

00:00:50.867 --> 00:00:53.733
Second, the proof library. In the
console: Settings, Proof library.

00:00:53.733 --> 00:00:56.767
It holds what you can back
up: case studies, metrics,

00:00:56.767 --> 00:00:59.267
testimonials and logos. Upload
a file or paste the text.

00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:00.600
Your model proposes the claims,

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.833
and each one must quote
the source word for word,

00:01:02.833 --> 00:01:05.900
with its numbers inside
the quote, or it's dropped.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.000
You review the claims
before anyone approves.

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:11.200
Set a permission on each source:
public, anonymized or internal only.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.233
A customer whose proof isn't
public can never be named,

00:01:14.233 --> 00:01:17.267
whatever the draft says. Set an
expiry date, then propose activation,

00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:18.867
and a different person approves.

00:01:18.867 --> 00:01:22.367
Only active proof backs a claim:
approved by a second person, not expired,

00:01:22.367 --> 00:01:23.700
and not internal only.

00:01:23.733 --> 00:01:25.533
Operator tip: make expiry a habit.

00:01:25.533 --> 00:01:29.200
Each source shows its state:
active, waiting for a second person,

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:29.867
or expired.

00:01:29.867 --> 00:01:34.033
At a glance you see what backs your
copy right now, and what to renew first.

00:01:34.067 --> 00:01:36.400
Third, the gate, the part
that makes this safe.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.733
Every factual claim in outbound content
is checked against active proof:

00:01:39.733 --> 00:01:42.433
a statistic, a result, a
named customer or an outcome.

00:01:42.433 --> 00:01:44.633
It's checked twice, when
the message is proposed,

00:01:44.633 --> 00:01:48.033
and again right before it's sent,
under the policy in force then.

00:01:48.033 --> 00:01:51.067
The gate covers outreach messages and
sequence steps, SEO page publishes,

00:01:51.067 --> 00:01:53.433
and meeting invites
and cancellation notes.

00:01:53.433 --> 00:01:56.133
If proof is archived or expires
between approval and send,

00:01:56.133 --> 00:01:58.433
the send fails rather
than going out unbacked.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.233
Each claim gets one of three verdicts.

00:02:00.233 --> 00:02:03.633
Backed by proof, with its source
shown to the approver. Close to proof,

00:02:03.633 --> 00:02:05.600
meaning the numbers and direction match an

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.800
approved claim and enough
of the wording does.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:10.267
And No proof. Gotcha: close
never counts as backed.

00:02:10.267 --> 00:02:13.400
You set the close match threshold
in the claims check settings.

00:02:13.433 --> 00:02:15.367
Then choose what happens
to an unbacked claim.

00:02:15.367 --> 00:02:18.867
In the console: Settings, Proof library,
Claims check. Block is the default:

00:02:18.867 --> 00:02:21.700
the proposal stops until the copy
is fixed or the proof is added.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:23.800
Route to the approver lets it through,

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:25.467
but the approver must acknowledge each

00:02:25.467 --> 00:02:27.400
claim in the Claims
panel before approving.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.733
The proposer, or an agent,
can't acknowledge for them.

00:02:29.733 --> 00:02:31.467
Setting this policy is human only.

00:02:31.533 --> 00:02:32.567
Or ask your agent:

00:02:32.567 --> 00:02:35.800
"Add this case study to the proof
library and propose it for activation".

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:37.067
It returns an approval number,

00:02:37.067 --> 00:02:39.500
and if you ask it to draft
and propose a message,

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:40.800
it reports the claims verdicts.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.533
It can't approve an activation, set
the claims policy, or approve a phrase.

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:45.967
Fourth, customer language.

00:02:45.967 --> 00:02:48.967
In the console: Settings, Proof
library, Customer language,

00:02:48.967 --> 00:02:49.400
then Find phrases.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:53.433
It reads your call transcripts,
classified replies and won deal notes,

00:02:53.433 --> 00:02:56.033
skips private calls, and lists
the words customers used.

00:02:56.033 --> 00:02:59.400
Each phrase shows its source, and a
person approves or rejects every one.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:02.133
Approved phrases ground drafts
in how your customers talk,

00:03:02.133 --> 00:03:04.867
and rejected ones are never
used. Or ask your agent:

00:03:04.867 --> 00:03:08.567
"Find customer phrases from our
recent calls and replies for review".

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:09.600
One honest limit.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.467
Drafts today use your brand voice,
your proof and your customers' phrases,

00:03:13.467 --> 00:03:15.567
but not yet a full company
context definition.

00:03:15.567 --> 00:03:18.700
For anything beyond those three,
a person reviews the draft.

00:03:18.767 --> 00:03:21.967
Here's the doubt: a gate that
blocks claims will stall outreach.

00:03:21.967 --> 00:03:24.200
It stops only factual
claims with no proof,

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:25.667
and you choose what happens next.

00:03:25.667 --> 00:03:28.400
Add the proof once, and
every later draft can use it.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.400
Or route to the approver, who
acknowledges each claim by name.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:33.867
Either way, nothing
unbacked goes out silently.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:36.433
Quick pass: Set your voice
and forbidden claims.

00:03:36.433 --> 00:03:39.000
Build proof with a
permission and an expiry.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.067
Let the gate check every claim, twice.

00:03:41.067 --> 00:03:45.000
Say only what you can prove, checked
when proposed and again when sent.

00:03:45.067 --> 00:03:48.133
Your voice, your proof,
and a gate that reads both,

00:03:48.133 --> 00:03:49.833
with a person on every exception.

00:03:49.833 --> 00:03:52.400
Add one real case study
to the library today.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:55.300
The next clip is consent,
suppression and privacy.
